WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.528
Programming languages are full of

00:00:02.595 --> 00:00:05.498
symbols that come in pairs: round brackets,

00:00:05.498 --> 00:00:07.794
square brackets, and curly braces.

00:00:07.861 --> 00:00:10.022
A compiler must check that every

00:00:10.089 --> 00:00:12.182
opening symbol is closed by the

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:15.220
matching closing symbol, in the right order.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.644
This little fragment is balanced.

00:00:18.644 --> 00:00:22.835
Keep only its symbols, in order: '(',

00:00:22.835 --> 00:00:26.375
')', '{', '['

00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:28.890
']', '}'.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.757
Here are some more examples.

00:00:31.757 --> 00:00:33.881
These three are balanced.

00:00:33.881 --> 00:00:35.597
These three are not:

00:00:35.597 --> 00:00:39.028
a square bracket that closes a round one,

00:00:39.109 --> 00:00:41.478
a closing square bracket with

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.357
nothing left to close,

00:00:43.357 --> 00:00:46.870
and a square bracket that is never closed.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.184
Counting is not enough.

00:00:49.184 --> 00:00:50.208
'(' ']' has one opener

00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:53.965
and one closer,

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:55.417
but it is wrong.

00:00:55.417 --> 00:00:59.259
And in '(', '[',

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:00.455
')', ']',

00:01:00.455 --> 00:01:03.187
each kind is counted correctly,

00:01:03.187 --> 00:01:04.980
but the pairs cross.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.592
So read the symbols from left to right.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:11.158
An opening symbol can never make

00:01:11.236 --> 00:01:13.568
the text unbalanced by itself;

00:01:13.568 --> 00:01:16.600
it is just waiting to be closed later.

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:20.453
A closing symbol must close the most recent

00:01:20.535 --> 00:01:23.305
opening symbol that is still open.

00:01:23.305 --> 00:01:23.794
Here,

00:01:23.794 --> 00:01:27.460
the ']' must match the '[',

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:30.231
the latest opener not yet closed.

00:01:30.231 --> 00:01:33.408
Then the '}' closes the '{',

00:01:33.408 --> 00:01:36.830
and the ')' closes the '('.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:39.137
The most recent opener that

00:01:39.209 --> 00:01:40.441
is still waiting—

00:01:40.441 --> 00:01:43.340
that is exactly what a stack gives you.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:45.799
Here is the algorithm.

00:01:45.799 --> 00:01:47.592
Make an empty stack,

00:01:47.592 --> 00:01:50.409
and read the symbols one by one.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:52.800
An opening symbol: push it.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.056
A closing symbol: if the stack is empty,

00:01:56.056 --> 00:01:57.074
return false,

00:01:57.074 --> 00:01:59.617
because there is nothing to close.

00:01:59.617 --> 00:02:02.886
Otherwise pop, and if the popped symbol does

00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:05.865
not match the closing one, return false.

00:02:05.865 --> 00:02:08.190
Any other character is ignored.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:09.393
At the end,

00:02:09.393 --> 00:02:12.106
return true if the stack is empty

00:02:12.106 --> 00:02:13.734
and false otherwise

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:16.060
some opener was never closed.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.362
Let's run it on ( { 

00:02:20.362 --> 00:02:24.179
 [ ] } 

00:02:24.179 --> 00:02:25.420
 ) .

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.138
( : push.

00:02:27.138 --> 00:02:28.856
{ : push.

00:02:28.856 --> 00:02:30.669
[ : push.

00:02:30.669 --> 00:02:34.200
Now three openers wait on the stack.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:38.143
] : pop the open square.

00:02:38.143 --> 00:02:39.411
It matches.

00:02:39.411 --> 00:02:43.849
} : pop the open curly, a match.

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:47.336
) : pop the open round

00:02:47.336 --> 00:02:48.710
a match too.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.329
At the end the stack is empty

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:53.220
so the text is balanced.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.038
Now you try.

00:02:55.038 --> 00:02:59.268
Trace these two: { ( 

00:02:59.268 --> 00:03:03.499
 ] [ ] 

00:03:03.499 --> 00:03:07.832
 ) } ; and ( 

00:03:07.832 --> 00:03:11.856
 ) [ [ 

00:03:11.856 --> 00:03:15.880
 ] { } .

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.125
Where does each one fail?

00:03:18.125 --> 00:03:20.550
Pause the video and trace them.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:25.488
The first one.

00:03:25.488 --> 00:03:27.234
{ : push.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:28.979
( : push.

00:03:28.979 --> 00:03:33.150
Then the close square pops the open round

00:03:33.150 --> 00:03:35.283
which does not match.

00:03:35.283 --> 00:03:37.902
Return false, immediately

00:03:37.902 --> 00:03:41.490
the rest of the input is never read.

00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:43.449
The second one.

00:03:43.449 --> 00:03:45.470
Open round is pushed

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:48.962
and the close round pops it: a match.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:52.454
Open square, open square: two pushes.

00:03:52.454 --> 00:03:55.303
Close square pops one of them.

00:03:55.303 --> 00:03:57.325
Open curly is pushed

00:03:57.325 --> 00:03:59.990
and the close curly pops it.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.867
Every closing symbol matched

00:04:02.954 --> 00:04:06.264
but at the end one open square bracket

00:04:06.351 --> 00:04:08.268
is still on the stack.

00:04:08.268 --> 00:04:10.620
So the text is unbalanced.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.722
So there are three ways to fail.

00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:15.850
A closing symbol that does

00:04:15.929 --> 00:04:17.900
not match the popped one

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:21.210
a closing symbol when the stack is empty

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.520
and a stack that is not empty at the end.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:25.719
In Java

00:04:25.719 --> 00:04:28.737
isBalanced takes the text as a string

00:04:28.737 --> 00:04:31.755
and uses our LinkedStack<Character>.

00:04:31.755 --> 00:04:34.540
It looks at each character in turn.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.287
An opening bracket is pushed.

00:04:37.287 --> 00:04:39.189
For a closing bracket,

00:04:39.271 --> 00:04:41.421
an empty stack means there

00:04:41.503 --> 00:04:44.314
is nothing to close: return false.

00:04:44.314 --> 00:04:46.794
Otherwise it pops the opener

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:49.605
and returns false if matches says

00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:52.250
the two are of different kinds.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.961
Other characters are simply ignored.

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:59.700
After the loop, the method returns whether

00:04:59.787 --> 00:05:01.439
the stack is empty;

00:05:01.526 --> 00:05:03.700
an opener left over makes

00:05:03.787 --> 00:05:05.526
the text unbalanced.

00:05:05.526 --> 00:05:09.265
And matches checks the three pairs: round,

00:05:09.265 --> 00:05:10.830
square and curly.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.246
The test program agrees with our traces.

00:05:14.246 --> 00:05:17.011
The three balanced examples give true.

00:05:17.011 --> 00:05:19.634
The five unbalanced ones give false,

00:05:19.705 --> 00:05:22.115
including our two Predict examples

00:05:22.186 --> 00:05:23.817
and the crossing pairs.

00:05:23.817 --> 00:05:25.943
The empty string is balanced,

00:05:25.943 --> 00:05:28.850
and so is the C fragment from the start.

00:05:29.430 --> 00:05:32.866
Each character is read once and causes

00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:35.489
at most one push or one pop,

00:05:35.579 --> 00:05:38.473
so the check runs in O(n) time

00:05:38.563 --> 00:05:41.005
for a text of n characters.

00:05:41.005 --> 00:05:43.898
In the worst case, all openers,

00:05:43.898 --> 00:05:46.702
the stack holds all n symbols,

00:05:46.702 --> 00:05:48.510
O(n) extra space.

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.140
Two classic bugs.

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.474
Returning true after the loop

00:05:52.474 --> 00:05:55.410
instead of whether the stack is empty

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.120
accepts the second Predict example

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.831
and even three open round brackets.

00:06:00.906 --> 00:06:04.445
And popping without checking for an empty stack

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.381
fails on a single close round bracket.

00:06:07.381 --> 00:06:09.037
Our pop returns null,

00:06:09.112 --> 00:06:11.823
and unboxing null into a char throws

00:06:11.898 --> 00:06:13.780
a NullPointerException.

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:17.175
A real compiler also skips brackets inside

00:06:17.248 --> 00:06:19.350
string literals and comments,

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:22.250
but the stack is the core of the check.

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:23.656
To sum up.

00:06:23.656 --> 00:06:27.125
One: a closing symbol must match the most

00:06:27.208 --> 00:06:29.933
recent opener that is still open,

00:06:29.933 --> 00:06:33.320
and a stack keeps exactly those openers.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:34.889
Two: push openers,

00:06:34.889 --> 00:06:37.367
a closer pops and must match,

00:06:37.367 --> 00:06:40.670
and the stack must be empty at the end.

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.231
Three: each character costs at

00:06:43.313 --> 00:06:45.048
most one push or pop

00:06:45.048 --> 00:06:47.030
so the check is O(n).

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:51.487
Try this one: [ (

00:06:51.487 --> 00:06:55.702
) ] {

00:06:55.702 --> 00:06:59.809
( } ).

00:06:59.809 --> 00:07:03.807
At which symbol does the check stop

00:07:03.807 --> 00:07:04.780
and why?

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.247
In part four

00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:09.771
a stack evaluates arithmetic expressions

00:07:09.857 --> 00:07:11.490
written in postfix.

